\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Two throats at rest, on the far-side-matched pairs (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:solve}): the sign rule (a,b), the force law (c) and its growth with throat width (d). (a)~Separation change of two matched $d=12$ pairs one sign apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=3/2$ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=5$ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:force}: measured $1.462\pm 0.022$ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<3.5$, excluded; open circles past $t=10.5$, where the closing pair has closed $4.6\%$ of its gap, excluded too. (c)~The force law on the ladder: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~The push against throat width, on logarithmic axes: how far like-signed $d=12$ pairs of width $a=1/1.5/2/3$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots; $a=2$ is the $d=12$ rung of (c)), against the point-charge $a^2$ of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:force} scaled through $a=2$ (grey, dashed) and a power law fitted to all four rungs (gold). If the push grew as $a^2$, every dot would lie on the grey line; the dots climb more slowly, as $a^{1.4}$.}}{11}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pair}{{5}{11}{Two throats at rest, on the far-side-matched pairs (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:solve}): the sign rule (a,b), the force law (c) and its growth with throat width (d). (a)~Separation change of two matched $d=\clmPairSeparation $ pairs one sign apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=\clmScalarQRatio $ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=\clmScalarQ $ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref {eq:force}: measured $\clmMatchedSignRatio \pm \clmMatchedSignRatioErr $ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<\clmSignSettleWindow $, excluded; open circles past $t=\clmSignWindowEnd $, where the closing pair has closed $\clmSignWindowClosed \%$ of its gap, excluded too. (c)~The force law on the ladder: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~The push against throat width, on logarithmic axes: how far like-signed $d=12$ pairs of width $a=1/1.5/2/3$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots; $a=2$ is the $d=12$ rung of (c)), against the point-charge $a^2$ of Eq.~\eqref {eq:force} scaled through $a=2$ (grey, dashed) and a power law fitted to all four rungs (gold). If the push grew as $a^2$, every dot would lie on the grey line; the dots climb more slowly, as $a^{\clmWidthExponentFit }$}{figure.5}{}}
